20282:
Recall Bohr energy relations where kinetic energy is positive and potential energy is negative with E = K + U
For Coulomb binding the virial result gives K = -E and U = 2E
Substituting E = -3.4\text{ eV} gives K = 3.4\text{ eV}
Then U = 2 \times (-3.4\text{ eV}) = -6.8\text{ eV}
→ (C)

20284:
Recall the drum wall supplies centripetal normal reaction N = m \omega^{2} r toward the axis
Vertical equilibrium needs static friction to balance weight with f = mg and limit f \le \mu N
At minimum speed \mu m \omega^{2} r = mg so \omega = \sqrt{\frac{g}{\mu r}}
With g = 10\text{ m/s}^{2} and \mu = 0.1 and r = 1\text{ m}, \omega = \sqrt{\frac{10}{0.1}} = 10\text{ rad/s}
→ (C)

20285:
Recall head-on elastic velocity v_{1}^{\prime} = \frac{m_{1} - m_{2}}{m_{1} + m_{2}} u for target at rest
Here m_{1} = 4m and m_{2} = 2m so v_{1}^{\prime} = \frac{4m - 2m}{4m + 2m} u = \frac{u}{3}
Retained kinetic fraction is \left(\frac{v_{1}^{\prime}}{u}\right)^{2} = \frac{1}{9}
Lost fraction is 1 - \frac{1}{9} = \frac{8}{9}
→ (B)

20289:
Recall induced emf magnitude e = N \frac{\Delta\Phi}{\Delta t} with flux \Phi = BA \cos\theta
Initially normal to field gives \Phi_{i} = BA and after 90^{\circ} rotation \Phi_{f} = 0
Change is \Delta\Phi = 0.05 \times 5 \times 10^{-5} = 2.5 \times 10^{-6}\text{ Wb}
With N = 800 and \Delta t = 0.1\text{ s}, e = \frac{800 \times 2.5 \times 10^{-6}}{0.1} = 0.02\text{ V}
→ (D)

20291:
Recall a soap film has two surfaces so excess pressure is \Delta P = \frac{4T}{R}
With T = 2.5 \times 10^{-2}\text{ N/m} and R = 10^{-3}\text{ m}, \Delta P = \frac{4 \times 2.5 \times 10^{-2}}{10^{-3}} = 100\text{ Pa}
Equate to hydrostatic column \Delta P = \rho g Z_{0}
Then Z_{0} = \frac{100}{10^{3} \times 10} = 0.01\text{ m} = 1\text{ cm}
→ (C)

20292:
Recall linear expansion \Delta L = L \alpha \Delta T for each rod
Independence of temperature means combined change vanishes so L_{\text{Cu}} \alpha_{\text{Cu}} = L_{\text{Al}} \alpha_{\text{Al}}
Thus L_{\text{Al}} = L_{\text{Cu}} \frac{\alpha_{\text{Cu}}}{\alpha_{\text{Al}}}
With 88\text{ cm} \times \frac{1.7 \times 10^{-5}}{2.2 \times 10^{-5}} = 68\text{ cm}
→ (D)

20295:
A rolling body carries translational energy of its centre plus rotational energy about the centre
For a disc the moment of inertia is I = \frac{1}{2}mR^{2} and rolling without slipping gives \omega = \frac{v}{R}
Total kinetic energy is K = \frac{1}{2}mv^{2} + \frac{1}{2}I\omega^{2} = \frac{1}{2}mv^{2} + \frac{1}{4}mv^{2} = \frac{3}{4}mv^{2}
With m = 100\text{ kg} and v = 20\text{ cm s}^{-1} = 0.2\text{ m s}^{-1}, K = \frac{3}{4} \times 100 \times 0.04 = 3\text{ J}
Work needed to stop the disc equals this total mechanical energy
→ (A)

20297:
Inside a uniform earth the gravity at depth d falls linearly as g^{\prime} = g\left(1 - \frac{d}{R}\right)
Half way down to the centre means depth d = \frac{R}{2}, so g^{\prime} = g\left(1 - \frac{1}{2}\right) = \frac{g}{2}
Weight is mg^{\prime}, hence it also halves from 200\text{ N} to 100\text{ N}
→ (D)

20299:
Initial angular speed is \omega_{0} = 3\text{ rpm} = \frac{3 \times 2\pi}{60} = \frac{\pi}{10}\text{ rad s}^{-1}
Moment of inertia of the solid cylinder about its axis is I = \frac{1}{2}mR^{2} = \frac{1}{2} \times 2 \times 0.04^{2} = 1.6 \times 10^{-3}\text{ kg m}^{2}
Angle turned before stopping is \theta = 2\pi\text{ revolutions} = 4\pi^{2}\text{ rad}, and \omega^{2} = \omega_{0}^{2} + 2\alpha\theta gives |\alpha| = \frac{\omega_{0}^{2}}{2\theta} = \frac{1}{800}\text{ rad s}^{-2}
Required torque magnitude is \tau = I|\alpha| = \frac{1.6 \times 10^{-3}}{800} = 2 \times 10^{-6}\text{ N m}
→ (A)

20300:
From the diagram the radius of the reference circle is 3\text{ m}, so oscillation amplitude is 3\text{ m}
Period of revolution is 4\text{ s}, giving angular frequency \omega = \frac{2\pi}{T} = \frac{\pi}{2}\text{ rad s}^{-1}
At t = 0 the particle is at the extreme y = +3\text{ m}, so the projection follows a cosine with zero initial phase
Hence y(t) = 3\cos\left(\frac{\pi t}{2}\right) with y in m
→ (D)

20303:
Let each bulb have resistance R, with three in parallel in section A and three in parallel in section B, the sections in series
With all glowing, R_{A} = \frac{R}{3} and R_{B} = \frac{R}{3}, so R_{eq} = \frac{2R}{3} and power P_{1} = \frac{E^{2}}{R_{eq}} = \frac{3E^{2}}{2R}
With two glowing in A and one in B, R_{A}^{\prime} = \frac{R}{2} and R_{B}^{\prime} = R, so R_{eq}^{\prime} = \frac{3R}{2} and P_{2} = \frac{2E^{2}}{3R}
Ratio of the two powers is \frac{P_{1}}{P_{2}} = \frac{3/2}{2/3} = \frac{9}{4}
→ (B)

20305:
Work by a varying force along y is W = \int F\text{ d}y
Here F = 20 + 10y, so W = \int_{0}^{1}(20 + 10y)\text{ d}y = \left[20y + 5y^{2}\right]_{0}^{1}
Evaluation gives W = 20 + 5 = 25\text{ J}
→ (C)

20309:
Two identical equi-convex lenses of focal length f in contact give \frac{1}{F_{1}} = \frac{1}{f} + \frac{1}{f}, so F_{1} = \frac{f}{2}
Glycerin of the same refractive index filling the gap forms a biconcave lens whose power cancels one glass lens, since \frac{1}{f_{g}} = -\frac{1}{f}
Total power becomes \frac{1}{F_{2}} = \frac{1}{f} - \frac{1}{f} + \frac{1}{f} = \frac{1}{f}, so F_{2} = f
Hence F_{1} : F_{2} = \frac{f}{2} : f = 1 : 2
→ (B)

20311:
Field of one infinite line charge at distance r is E = \frac{\lambda}{2\pi\varepsilon_{0} r}
Mid-point is R from each wire since the wires are 2R apart
Field of the +\lambda wire points away from it and field of the -\lambda wire points toward it
Both fields hence point the same way across the gap and add up
Total E = \frac{\lambda}{2\pi\varepsilon_{0} R} + \frac{\lambda}{2\pi\varepsilon_{0} R} = \frac{\lambda}{\pi\varepsilon_{0} R}
→ (C)

20314:
Conduction current in the leads is I_{c} = C \frac{dV}{dt}
Capacitance C = 20\mu\text{F} = 20 \times 10^{-6}\text{F} with rate 3\text{ V/s}
Substitution gives I_{c} = 20 \times 10^{-6} \times 3 = 60 \times 10^{-6}\text{ A}
Maxwell displacement current I_{d} always equals the conduction current in charging
Both currents are therefore equal, matching the pair stated in option B
→ (B)

20317:
De Broglie wavelength of an electron accelerated through V is \lambda = \frac{12.27}{\sqrt{V}}\text{ \AA}
Here V = 10000\text{ V} so \sqrt{V} = 100
Division gives \lambda = 0.1227\text{ \AA} = 0.1227 \times 10^{-10}\text{ m}
This equals 12.27 \times 10^{-12}\text{ m}, nearly 12.2 \times 10^{-12}\text{ m}
→ (B)

20318:
Up the smooth incline retardation is a = g\sin\theta with launch speed v
Stopping distance from v^{2} = 2ax is x = \frac{v^{2}}{2g\sin\theta}
Same v gives x_{1} = \frac{v^{2}}{2g\sin 60^{\circ}} and x_{2} = \frac{v^{2}}{2g\sin 30^{\circ}}
Ratio \frac{x_{1}}{x_{2}} = \frac{\sin 30^{\circ}}{\sin 60^{\circ}} = \frac{1/2}{\sqrt{3}/2} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}
→ (C)

20319:
Efflux speed follows Torricelli law v = \sqrt{2gh}
Height h = 2\text{ m} with g = 10\text{ m/s}^{2} gives v = \sqrt{40} \approx 6.32\text{ m/s}
Hole area A = 2\text{ mm}^{2} = 2 \times 10^{-6}\text{ m}^{2}
Volume rate Q = Av \approx 2 \times 10^{-6} \times 6.32 = 12.64 \times 10^{-6}\text{ m}^{3}\text{/s}
→ (A)

20321:
Radius in uniform field B for momentum p is r = \frac{p}{qB}
Momenta are equal so radius is inversely proportional to charge
Ionized hydrogen carries q_{\text{H}} = e while \alpha carries q_{\alpha} = 2e
Ratio \frac{r_{\text{H}}}{r_{\alpha}} = \frac{2e}{e} = \frac{2}{1}
→ (A)

20323:
Work equals gain in potential with U(r) = -\frac{GMm}{r} from earth centre
Surface term is -\frac{GMm}{R} and height R gives -\frac{GMm}{2R}
Difference W = -\frac{GMm}{2R} + \frac{GMm}{R} = \frac{GMm}{2R}
Using GM = gR^{2} gives W = \frac{mgR}{2}
→ (C)

20324:
Angular width of the first minimum scales with wavelength in the medium
Immersion changes wavelength to \lambda^{\prime} = \frac{\lambda}{\mu}
Relative index \mu_{\text{water}} = \frac{4}{3} shrinks every fringe width by this factor
New width \theta^{\prime} = \frac{0.2^{\circ}}{4/3} = 0.15^{\circ}
→ (B)

20325:
For an irreversible expansion against constant external pressure the work in chemistry sign convention is w = -\text{P}_{ext}\Delta \text{V}
Volume change is \Delta \text{V} = 0.25 - 0.1 = 0.15\text{ L} with \text{P}_{ext} = 2\text{ bar}
Hence w = -2 \times 0.15 = -0.3\text{ L bar}
Using 1\text{ L bar} = 100\text{ J} gives w = -0.3 \times 100 = -30\text{ J}
→ (A)

20327:
pH 9 gives pOH = 14 - 9 = 5, so [\text{OH}^{-}] = 1 \times 10^{-5}\text{ M}
\text{Ca}(\text{OH})_{2} releases two hydroxides per unit, so solubility s = 0.5 \times 10^{-5}\text{ M}
K_{sp} = [\text{Ca}^{2+}][\text{OH}^{-}]^{2} = s(2s)^{2} = 4s^{3} = 4 \times (0.5 \times 10^{-5})^{3} = 0.5 \times 10^{-15}
This is option B
→ (B)

20329:
An ideal solution obeys Raoult law with like and unlike interactions equal
Volume change on mixing is zero with \Delta_{\text{mix}}\text{V} = 0 at constant \text{T} and \text{P}
Enthalpy change on mixing is zero with \Delta_{\text{mix}}\text{H} = 0 at constant \text{T} and \text{P}
Entropy of mixing is positive and Gibbs energy of mixing is negative so only the enthalpy statement matches
→ (C)

20330:
For a cell reaction the standard potential links to equilibrium by \text{E}^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = \frac{0.059}{\text{n}}\log \text{K}
Here \text{n} = 1 and \text{E}^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = 0.59\text{ V} at 298\text{ K}
Substituting gives 0.59 = 0.059 \log \text{K} so \log \text{K} = 10
Hence \text{K} = 1.0 \times 10^{10}
→ (C)

20335:
Freezing depression follows \Delta \text{T}_{\text{f}} = \text{K}_{\text{f}} \times \text{m}
Given \text{K}_{\text{f}} = 5.12\text{ K kg mol}^{-1} and molality 0.078
Multiplying gives \Delta \text{T}_{\text{f}} = 5.12 \times 0.078 = 0.399 \approx 0.40\text{ K}
This is option C
→ (C)

20347:
For first order kinetics \text{ln}\frac{[\text{R}]_{0}}{[\text{R}]} = \text{k}\text{t}
After 99 percent completion only 1 percent remains, so the ratio is 100
Converting to base ten logarithms gives \text{t} = \frac{2.303}{\text{k}}\text{log}100
Since \text{log}100 = 2, the time becomes \text{t} = \frac{2 \times 2.303}{\text{k}}
Evaluating gives \text{t} = \frac{4.606}{\text{k}}
→ (C)

20364:
Compressibility factor is defined as Z = \frac{pV_{m}}{RT} which equals \frac{V_{m,\text{real}}}{V_{m,\text{ideal}}}
Molar volume 20 percent smaller means V_{m,\text{real}} = 0.8 V_{m,\text{ideal}}
Division gives Z = 0.8 which is less than unity so the gas is more compressible than ideal
Such negative deviation arises when attractive forces dominate over repulsive forces
→ (C)

20444:
Cardiac output equals heart rate multiplied by stroke volume, with \text{CO} = \text{HR} \times \text{SV}
Stroke volume is end diastolic volume minus end systolic volume, so \text{SV} = 100 \text{ mL} - 50 \text{ mL} = 50 \text{ mL} = 0.05 \text{ L}
Heart rate is then \text{HR} = 5 \text{ L} / 0.05 \text{ L} = 100 beats per minute
→ (C)
